# ── Vital environment variables ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# Copy this file to `.env` and fill in real values there.
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# `.env` is gitignored and must never be committed: it is where secrets live.
# Everything below is SERVER-SIDE ONLY (no NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix), so none of these
# values can reach the browser bundle. Placeholders only — never a real token.
#
# With nothing set, Vital runs in demo mode over the committed fixtures.

# ── Host / container ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Host port the container is published on (container always listens on 3000).
# Change it if 8080 is taken:  VITAL_PORT=9090 docker compose up -d
VITAL_PORT=8080

# Node heap cap for the container runtime, in MB. Keep it small on tight hosts.
NODE_OPTIONS=--max-old-space-size=384

# Timezone calendar days are cut on and overnight sleep is assigned to.
# VITAL_TIMEZONE=UTC

# ── Data source ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# `live`  — read the Health Auto Export history, server-side, on every request
#           (cached in-process). The browser never calls this API and never sees
#           the token. If the source cannot be read, the app shows a connection
#           error; it never substitutes demo data.
# `demo`  — serve the committed fixtures (src/data/health-fixtures.json).
# Leave unset for demo mode.
# VITAL_DATA_MODE=live

# ── Health Auto Export integration ────────────────────────────────────────────

# Base URL of the Health Auto Export API. Read server-side and used for
# GET /api/metrics/:metric and GET /api/workouts.
# HAE_API_URL=http://localhost:3001

# Read token, sent as the `api-key` header on every upstream request. Server-side
# only: it must never appear in source, fixtures, the client bundle or a log.
# HAE_API_KEY=replace-with-your-read-token

# Metric the pipeline panel probes (over a bounded one-week window) before it
# marks the live stages healthy. Defaults to resting_heart_rate.
# HAE_PROBE_METRIC=resting_heart_rate

# How long a fetched, normalized dataset is cached in the server process, in
# seconds. Concurrent page loads share one upstream pass, and the cache is warmed
# once at process start. Once this TTL lapses the held dataset is served stale
# while one background refresh runs, so a page never waits for the rebuild.
# Defaults to 300.
# HAE_CACHE_TTL_SECONDS=300

# ── Profile ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The profile is NOT configured with environment variables: it is a JSON file the
# server owns and writes, so it is the same for every browser and both the
# greeting and the briefing read one record.
#
#   ./data/profile.json          on the host
#   /app/data/profile.json       inside the container (docker-compose mounts ./data)
#
# Fields: name, dateOfBirth (YYYY-MM-DD), notes, timezone (IANA), briefingHour
# (0-23). All optional; no credential and no health record belongs in it. It is
# edited in Settings → Account, or by editing the file directly.
# When the file does not exist the documented defaults are used and nothing
# crashes. data/profile.example.json is the shape reference.
#
# Optional: put the file somewhere else (it must be writable by the server process
# and should be a mounted volume, or the contents are lost on redeploy).
# VITAL_PROFILE_PATH=/app/data/profile.json
#
# The host directory the container writes into must be writable by uid 1001:
#   mkdir -p data && chmod 777 data

# ── AI analyst ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Five configurable things: provider, model, endpoint, credential, system prompt.
# SERVER-SIDE ONLY (no NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix), so none of these can reach the
# browser bundle. Placeholders only — never put a real key in this file.
#
# With ANALYST_PROVIDER unset (or `demo`) the analyst is a deterministic engine
# over the committed dataset: no provider, no credential, nothing sent anywhere.

# `demo` | `openai` | `anthropic`. Default: demo.
#   openai    = ANY OpenAI-compatible /chat/completions endpoint: OpenAI,
#               OpenRouter, LM Studio, llama.cpp, vLLM, Ollama, Together, …
#   anthropic = the Anthropic Messages API (/v1/messages)
ANALYST_PROVIDER=demo

# Model id sent to the provider. Required whenever a remote provider is set.
# ANALYST_MODEL=replace-with-a-model-id

# Endpoint: a base URL (the provider's default path is appended) or a full
# endpoint. A URL already ending in /chat/completions (openai) or /v1/messages
# (anthropic) is used as-is.
#   OpenAI       https://api.openai.com/v1
#   OpenRouter   https://openrouter.ai/api/v1
#   LM Studio    http://host.docker.internal:1234/v1    (no key needed)
#   Ollama       http://host.docker.internal:11434/v1   (no key needed)
# ANALYST_API_URL=http://host.docker.internal:1234/v1

# Credential. Sent as `Authorization: Bearer` (openai) or `x-api-key`
# (anthropic). Required for any endpoint that is not on this machine; OPTIONAL
# for loopback hosts so a local model server works with no credential.
# Loopback hosts are: 127.0.0.1, localhost, [::1], host.docker.internal.
# ANALYST_API_KEY=replace-with-your-provider-key

# System prompt. ANALYST_SYSTEM_PROMPT_FILE WINS over the inline value when both
# are set. The file is re-read at request time when its mtime changes, so editing
# it takes effect on the next question. The container mounts ./config read-only
# at /app/config, and config/analyst-prompt.md is committed as a starting point.
# When neither is set the built-in prompt is used.
# ANALYST_SYSTEM_PROMPT=You are a laconic analyst. Answer only from the context.
# ANALYST_SYSTEM_PROMPT_FILE=/app/config/analyst-prompt.md

# Request tuning (defaults shown).
# ANALYST_MAX_TOKENS=1200
# ANALYST_TEMPERATURE=0.2
# ANALYST_TIMEOUT_MS=60000

# A configured provider is called directly: this single-user private deployment
# has no per-request permission prompt. GET /api/analyst, Settings → AI privacy
# and the Analyst page name the provider, model and destination host.

# `auto` (default) sends response_format {"type":"json_object"} and retries once
# without it on HTTP 400. `off` never sends it. Anthropic has no such field.
# ANALYST_JSON_MODE=auto

# ── Today's briefing ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The Overview hero is written by a model when one is reachable, and computed
# from the dataset when none is. Either way the hero states which it is:
#   `Written by <model>`  or  `Computed from your data — analyst model offline`.
#
# Engine precedence:
#   1. VITAL_LLM_BASE_URL — a model server on this machine. It is probed with a
#      bounded GET /models (~2 s); if it does not answer, the briefing falls
#      through to ANALYST_* instead of failing.
#   2. the ANALYST_* provider above.
#   3. nothing — the computed briefing is shown, and no request leaves the host.
#
# From inside the container the host is `host.docker.internal` (docker-compose
# maps it). A loopback endpoint needs no credential.
# VITAL_LLM_BASE_URL=http://host.docker.internal:1234/v1
# `auto` takes the first model id the server advertises; anything else is used
# verbatim as the model id.
# VITAL_LLM_MODEL=auto
# Optional; only needed if the local server requires a bearer token.
# VITAL_LLM_API_KEY=

# The fallback provider (optional). If the preferred engine's reply fails the
# number guard — a fabricated figure, or a figure the context records under a
# different metric — the text is discarded and re-asked here ONCE, then never
# again. If it is unset or unusable, the computed briefing is shown instead and
# the payload says so. Any OpenAI-compatible /chat/completions endpoint works;
# OpenRouter (a hosted model) is the usual choice.
# VITAL_LLM_FALLBACK_BASE_URL=https://openrouter.ai/api/v1
# VITAL_LLM_FALLBACK_MODEL=deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash
# VITAL_LLM_FALLBACK_API_KEY=

# There is deliberately NO briefing cache TTL to set. A briefing's cache key is
# the local calendar day (in the profile's timezone) plus the unit system, so one
# briefing is written per day and reused for that whole day; new observations
# arriving during the day do not regenerate it. A new day's briefing is written
# lazily, by the first request at/after the profile's briefingHour — there is no
# scheduler and no background job. `Regenerate` on the hero replaces the current
# day's briefing on demand.

# Request tuning for the briefing call (defaults shown). It is intentionally
# shorter than the analyst's, because the briefing runs in the background.
# A reasoning model spends BRIEFING_MAX_TOKENS on reasoning AND its reply, so
# the default is generous: truncation arrives as a cut-off JSON object.
# BRIEFING_MAX_TOKENS=4000
# BRIEFING_TEMPERATURE=0.3
# BRIEFING_TIMEOUT_MS=60000

# ── Postgres: configuration, profile and (later) analyst conversations ────────
# NOT health data. No observations, metric series or workouts are ever stored in
# this database; health history stays with the Health Auto Export source.
#
# Generate these once with `npm run db:init` (scripts/init-env.sh): it creates a
# strong password the first time, appends anything missing, and never overwrites
# an existing value. `docker compose up -d --build` then starts the database.
#
# HOST-SIDE values: used by psql and `npm run db:migrate` run on this machine.
# 5432 is deliberately NOT used — another Postgres on this host already owns it.
VITAL_PG_HOST=127.0.0.1
VITAL_PG_PORT=5433
VITAL_PG_DATABASE=vital
VITAL_PG_USER=vital
VITAL_PG_PASSWORD=          # generated by `npm run db:init`; never committed
VITAL_PG_SSL=false
#
# A full connection string wins over the parts above when both are set.
# DATABASE_URL=postgres://vital:password@127.0.0.1:5433/vital
#
# INSIDE the compose network the app connects to the service directly, so
# docker-compose.yml passes it separate values (host `vital-postgres`, port
# 5432). Override only if you rename the service or move the database off-host:
# VITAL_PG_INTERNAL_HOST / VITAL_PG_INTERNAL_PORT.
#
# With none of these set, the app runs on its JSON files exactly as before.
